[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65900,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rhabdomyoma is a benign mesenchymal neoplasm showing skeletal muscle differentiation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65901,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8905/0 Genital rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65902,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E86.2 &amp; XH5AF2 Rhabdomyoma &amp; Genital rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65903,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> genital rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65904,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65905,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cases arise in the vagina, whereas a smaller subset of cases involve the vulva or cervix.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65906,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital rhabdomyomas are often asymptomatic and discovered during clinical examination for other reasons {22325314}. When symptomatic, these lesions can cause dyspareunia, bleeding, or urinary tract compression.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65907,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital rhabdomyoma is the least common subtype of extracardiac rhabdomyoma and generally occurs in women aged 30&ndash;55&nbsp;years {22325314; 28700439}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65908,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In contrast to fetal rhabdomyomas, genital rhabdomyomas have not been reported in association with basal cell naevus syndrome.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65909,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Immunohistochemical analysis of 1&nbsp;case revealed evidence of activation of the AKT/mTOR signalling pathway {26247533}. A genome-wide microarray analysis did not detect somatic copy-number alterations or allelic loss events in 2&nbsp;cases of genital rhabdomyoma {28700439}. There was no evidence of somatic changes involving hedgehog pathway genes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65910,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rhabdomyoma is typically a solitary polypoid or nodular lesion, usually &lt;&nbsp;3&nbsp;cm, with a grey, rubbery to glistening cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65911,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These lesions are characterized by a subepithelial proliferation of spindle, ovoid, or strap-shaped cells arranged in a haphazard pattern {28700439}. The tumour cells have abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm with distinct cross-striations. Mitoses and nuclear pleomorphism are absent. The cells are positive for desmin and markers of skeletal muscle differentiation, including myogenin and MYOD1.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65912,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65913,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65914,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> subepithelial demarcated proliferation of spindled to ovoid cells with a haphazard pattern; variable numbers of strap-shaped cells with cross-striations; no atypia or mitoses.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> skeletal muscle marker positivity (in selected cases).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65915,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65916,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 435,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are benign tumours and are generally cured by local excision {22325314; 28700439}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:31:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]